{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "\"To access the print version of the article, a link is provided. The rules for the Sudoku puzzle are explained, with a key exception: cells within grey lines must sum up to the numbers in the corner. This variation adds a new layer of complexity to the traditional game. The usual rule of not repeating numbers within each shape still applies, requiring careful consideration from players.\"\n\n\n \nAddition to the standard Sudoku rules is made with the introduction of grey lines, which contain cells that must add up to specific figures. These figures are located in the corners, serving as a guide for players as they fill in the puzzle. The constraint of not repeating numbers within each shape formed by grey lines remains in place, ensuring the game requires strategic thinking.\n\n\n \nThe Sudoku puzzle is presented with a unique twist, incorporating grey lines that delineate specific areas. Within these areas, the numbers must sum up to the values indicated in the corners. This adds an extra level of challenge, as players must balance the traditional Sudoku rules with this new requirement. The game demands attention to detail and logical reasoning.\n\n\n \nFor those who enjoy puzzles and games, the Sudoku presented offers a stimulating challenge. With its modified rules and intricate layout, it provides an engaging activity for those looking to exercise their problem-solving skills. The presence of grey lines and corner figures introduces a fresh element, setting it apart from the standard version of the game.\n\n\n \nThe completed puzzle will be available in next week's Observer Digital Edition, allowing readers to check their work or simply appreciate the solution. In the meantime, the puzzle remains a fun and intellectual challenge, inviting players to test their ski